-- Rename colors -> products, rename order_items color_* -> product_*,
-- drop the now-unused image_key column, and update the three product
-- stories with long-form copy. 001 and 002 are already applied and are
-- left unchanged.

RENAME TABLE colors TO products;

ALTER TABLE order_items
  RENAME COLUMN color_id TO product_id,
  RENAME COLUMN color_name TO product_name;

ALTER TABLE products
  DROP COLUMN image_key;

UPDATE products SET story = 'I made this one on an evening in late October, when the light had gone thin and gold and the studio windows turned the color of weak tea. That last hour before dusk does something to a room: it slows everything down, and every surface it touches looks like it''s remembering something. I wanted a bead that could hold that hour past sunset, so you could carry it into a morning that has none of that softness in it.

Amber isn''t really one color. Hold a bead up and it shifts from deep honey to pale citrine depending on how the light finds it, the way real amber does when it''s held up to a window. I chose beads with visible facets on purpose, because facets catch light from more directions than a smooth round bead ever could. Turn your wrist an inch and the whole bracelet seems to change temperature. That was the whole point: something that keeps a little warmth in reserve, so it''s there when the room around you isn''t.

You know the mornings I mean. The ones where you wake up already tired of the day before it''s started, where the coffee doesn''t help and the light through the blinds is flat and grey. Courage on mornings like that never shows up as anything dramatic. Nobody strides into a hard day fully armored. Mostly you just find some small, private thing to hold onto — a song, a text from a friend, the memory of a good laugh — and you use it to get from the door to the car and from the car to whatever''s waiting. I wanted this bracelet to be that kind of small thing. Not a talisman that promises to fix anything, just a warm weight against your wrist that says: you''ve felt good light before, and you will again, and here''s a little of it, worn close enough to touch.

There''s a particular kind of courage that never gets much credit because it doesn''t look like anything from the outside. It''s not the courage of grand gestures. It''s the courage of showing up anyway — to the meeting you''re dreading, the conversation you''ve been putting off, the ordinary Tuesday that asks more than it should. That kind of courage doesn''t roar. It doesn''t need to. It just needs to be carried, quietly, the way you''d carry a hand warmer in a coat pocket on a cold day, taken out only when you need it, put away again when you don''t.

I think about that a lot when I''m stringing these beads — how something this small can do a job that has nothing to do with size. A bracelet can''t change your circumstances. It can''t make the hard conversation easier or the deadline further away. But it can be a private cue, a little clasp of warmth against your pulse, reminding you that the last hour of sun exists somewhere even when you can''t see it from where you''re standing. Amber has always seemed to me like sunlight that decided to stay solid a while longer, to not disappear the second the sun went down. I like the idea of wearing a piece of that decision.

Some people tell me they touch the bracelet before a hard call, turning it once around their wrist like a small ritual. Others forget it''s there until a bead catches the light on the train home, and they remember, for a second, why they put it on that morning. Both uses feel right to me. This isn''t jewelry that needs to be looked at to work. It just needs to be there, holding its own temperature, the way the sky holds onto gold long after the sun has technically set.

So here''s what I hope for it, more than anything: that you put it on before the hard morning, not after the good day. That you let it be ordinary most of the time and quietly useful on the days that need it. Wear it low on your wrist, where you''ll catch it in your own periphery — a small, private hour of sun, however grey the morning turns out to be.' WHERE slug = 'sunlit-amber';

UPDATE products SET story = 'This one started with a hike I almost turned back from. It had rained for three days straight and the trail was more mud than path, and I remember standing under the canopy watching water drip off a branch onto a bed of moss so thick it looked upholstered. Everything smelled like wet bark and something mineral underneath it, like the ground itself was breathing. I didn''t turn back. I sat there for a while instead, looking at how green a forest gets right after rain — not one green, dozens, moss and lichen and old bark and new leaf all slightly different shades of the same idea. I wanted a bead that held that particular green, the deep, wet, unglamorous kind that most people walk past without noticing.

Because that''s the thing about a forest floor: nothing about it looks like it''s doing much. There''s no visible motion, no obvious change from one week to the next. But underneath, everything is happening. Roots are finding water. Seeds are deciding whether this is the year. Fungus is quietly breaking down a fallen limb into the soil that will feed something else entirely. Growth in a forest is almost never a performance. It''s private, slow, and mostly invisible, and it happens whether or not anyone''s there to see it.

I think that''s the kind of growth most of us are actually doing, most of the time. Not the dramatic before-and-after kind you''d put in a photo. The kind where you can''t point to the exact day you got better at something, or the exact moment you stopped being who you used to be — you just look up one day and notice the ground under you has changed, quietly, while you were busy living on it. Patience is the only ingredient that matters for that kind of growth, and patience is hard, because it asks you to trust a process you can''t watch happening.

Emerald felt like the right color for that kind of trust. Not the bright, saturated green you''d use to celebrate something loud — a deeper, mossier tone, closer to what the forest actually looks like than what a postcard of it looks like. I like beads with a slight matte finish for this one, because moss doesn''t shine. It just sits there, soft and certain, doing its slow work without asking to be noticed.

Renewal doesn''t always look like starting over from nothing, either. Sometimes it looks exactly like the beginning of this story — standing in the rain, tired, ready to turn back, and choosing to sit down in the wet instead and let something register. A forest after a storm isn''t clean or triumphant. It''s soaked and heavy and a little bit of a mess, and it''s also, unmistakably, alive in a way it wasn''t the day before. I think new chapters mostly look like that. Not tidy. Not obviously better yet. Just wetter, heavier, and quietly full of everything that''s about to grow.

I made this bracelet for the people who are in that part of the story — the soaked, unglamorous middle, where you can''t see what you''re growing into yet but you can feel that something under the surface has shifted. Not the finish line. The moss stage. The stage where the only proof of change is that you''re still here, still rooted, still turning rain into something usable one slow day at a time.

Put it on when you''re marking something you can''t quite explain yet — a decision you made, a version of yourself you''re leaving in the mud behind you, a chapter that hasn''t announced its name. You don''t need to know the ending. Wear it the way the forest wears its rain: quietly, and get on with the growing.' WHERE slug = 'forest-emerald';

UPDATE products SET story = 'The idea for this one came from wading, not swimming. There''s a particular stretch of coast I go back to every summer, where the water stays shallow for a long way out over pale sand, so pale and so shallow that the sea there looks more blue-white than blue, almost the color of skimmed milk with sunlight thrown into it. You can walk out further than seems reasonable and the water only reaches your knees. It''s only past a certain point that the sand drops away and the color changes all at once, shallow pale blue giving way to something deeper and more serious, the real sea, the one with weight to it. I wanted a bead that could hold both of those blues in one piece — the shallow, forgiving kind and the deep, steady kind underneath it.

Topaz does that naturally, if you find the right stones. Held one way it''s almost clear, catching light the way sand-bright water does. Tilt it and it deepens, the way the sea does the moment you''re out past your depth. I liked that a single bead could hold that whole walk from shore to open water, from the part where you can still see your own feet to the part where you have to trust what''s under you without being able to see it.

Clarity, to me, isn''t really about seeing everything. It''s closer to that shallow stretch of water — being able to see clearly for as far as you actually need to, and trusting the rest. You don''t need to see the whole ocean floor to know how to swim. You just need to trust the tide, and the tide is the most reliable thing in the whole system. It comes in. It goes out. It doesn''t skip a day because it''s tired, and it doesn''t rush because you''re anxious. It just keeps doing the same quiet, enormous thing on a schedule older than any of us, and somehow that''s more reassuring than almost anything else I know.

I think about that steadiness on the days that don''t have any in them — the days that are all noise, all overlapping demands, everyone needing something at once, no clean sequence to any of it. On days like that I think about standing in shallow water and feeling the pull of a wave receding around your ankles, small but insistent, and how your body just adjusts to it without being asked. You don''t fight the tide. You let it move through you and past you, and you''re still standing when it''s done. That''s most of what calm actually is, in my experience — not the absence of waves, just knowing they''ll pass, and letting them.

Breathing out is different from breathing in. Breathing in takes effort; breathing out is mostly just permission, letting go of something you were already holding. That''s the moment I picture most with this bracelet — not the deep breath before something hard, but the exhale after, on a crowded train or in a loud room or halfway through a day that''s asked too much of you already. A small blue weight on your wrist, cool even in a warm room, there to remind you that somewhere the tide is still moving in its unhurried way regardless of how your day is going.

I don''t think Ocean Topaz is meant for the big dramatic swim out past the waves. It''s meant for the ordinary wade — the daily walk out into water that''s mostly shallow and occasionally deep, where you need just enough clarity to take the next step and just enough calm to trust the ground when you can''t see it anymore.

So wear it on the loud days, the ones with no clean edges. Let it be the small blue reminder, against your own pulse, that the tide always comes back in.' WHERE slug = 'ocean-topaz';
